TEST 38475 -- ATMF - recover from USB drive
===========================================================================
Run:    2026-09-23 (lab date; device clocks 2026-09-23 00:26-00:47 UTC), tb470
Build:  awplus_main-20260923-20 on the DUT and the master
DUT:    IE520-sa -- the STANDALONE IE520 (S/N 264A23068, /dev/u3), AMF MEMBER.
        It is the unit that used to be stack member 2; it still held STACK ID 2
        (ports port2.0.x) when this case started.
Master: IE520-stk (3-member stack), AMF master of network "tb470"
USB:    28.9 GB stick in the DUT (Terrence moved it there 2026-09-23)
Case precondition: "DUT requires virtual-link for this test"

VERDICT: FAIL at step 5 -- "Automatic node recovery failed - user intervention
         required". Steps 1-4 PASS. The DUT DID find and apply the recovery file on
         the USB drive, but came back without its virtual link, so it could not reach
         a master. Two confounds and one candidate root cause are recorded below;
         n = 1, so a clean repeat is recommended before this is filed as a defect.
         (2026-09-21: UNMEASURED -- `atmf cleanup` refused on a VCStack. Running it
         on the standalone is what made this attempt possible.)

PRECONDITION -- virtual link as the DUT's ONLY AMF path                 done
  DUT:    atmf virtual-link id 1 ip 10.38.215.41 remote-id 2 remote-ip 10.38.215.40
  Master: atmf virtual-link id 2 ip 10.38.215.40 remote-id 1 remote-ip 10.38.215.41
  Then `no switchport atmf-link` + `switchport mode access` on sa3 at BOTH ends, so
  the L2 AMF link was gone and the tunnel carried AMF alone:
    master: vlink2  2 10.38.215.40  1 10.38.215.41  Tunnel State Complete
            show atmf links -> vlink2 Downlink Full IE520-sa Forwarding; nodes 4
    DUT:    vlink1 Uplink Full IE520-stk Forwarding; Role Member; nodes 4

STEP 1 -- "The DUT should be a member of an AMF network"                PASS
  IE520-sa: Network Name tb470, Role Member, Current ATMF Nodes 4.

STEP 2 -- "Insert a USB drive into the DUT"                              PASS
  show file systems: usbstick 28.9G, usb:, Avail Y.
  (`show atmf backup` on a member answers "% Error: Device configuration does not
  support ATMF backup commands." -- a master-only command, not a fault.)

STEP 3 -- "Save the configuration ... should create a recovery file on the USB.
           Recover file should be present on the USB drive"              PASS (see note)
  dir usb:  atmf_recovery_file 2272 B 00:26:40, atmf_recovery_file_hash 16 B 00:26:54
  The file IS present. NOTE -- it was written at 00:26:40, i.e. when the virtual
  link was configured, NOT by the save: two saves (`copy running-config
  startup-config` ~00:28:45 and `write` 00:29:23, then 90 s of polling) left its
  date and size unchanged. That matches the documented behaviour ("AMF recovery
  files are created for nodes with special links" -- virtual links, area links)
  rather than the case text's "the save creates it".
  `show atmf recovery-file` just before the wipe:
      DUT (IE520-sa):     "Special Link Not Present"  (!)  USB 00:26:40, local 00:22:34
      master (IE520-stk): "Special Link Present"  USB Media Not Found, local /
                          4050-5g / x230-10GP copies 00:22:36
  The DUT reporting NO special link while its virtual link was Full is the
  candidate root cause for step 5 -- see FINDINGS.

STEP 4 -- "Enter the command atmf cleanup"                               PASS
      IE520-sa# atmf cleanup
      This command will erase all NVS, all flash contents except for
      the boot release, a GUI resource file, and installed licenses,
      and then reboot the device.
      Proceed ? (y/n): y                                   (00:33:19)
  Rebooted (BootROM 1.41, bootloader master-20260822-535, IE520-tb470.rel):
      "Warning: flash:/default.cfg does not exist, loading factory defaults."
      "awplus login:" at t+196 s. Flash afterwards: only IE520-tb470.rel, the GUI
      file and log/ -- the erase did what it says.
  Safety step taken first: one sa3 leg shut on the master side (port4.0.9), so the
  wiped DUT could not form an asymmetric LAG with the stack.

STEP 5 -- "When the DUT reboots it should recover its configuration file
           from the USB drive"                                           FAIL
  DUT log (device clock):
      00:34:55  Updating ATMF backup location to "usb"
      00:37:12  atmffsd: ATMF recovery file detected in external media
      00:37:12  atmffsd: ATMF recovery file successfully retrieved
      00:37:12  atmffsd: Unable to remove recovery link                 <-- user.err
      00:37:38  Host name change [host_84e3_2787_0780 -> IE520-sa]
      00:38:43  Enabling all ports
      00:40:13  atmffsd: Recovery file processing successful, attempting to recover
                as IE520-sa
      00:40:13  atmffsd: Checking controller and master node availability
      00:45:13  atmffsd: Failed to find any master nodes
      00:45:13  atmffsd: Automatic node recovery failed - user intervention required
  What the DUT came back with (show running-config, 00:42):
      RESTORED   hostname IE520-sa, manager credentials, vlan1 10.38.215.41/27,
                 atmf network-name tb470, stack virtual-chassis-id 3439,
                 sa3 = port2.0.9 + port2.0.13 as a TRUNK with switchport atmf-link
      MISSING    the virtual link -- `show atmf virtual-links`: "Virtual Links
                 Configured: 0". It is the ONLY path this case gives the DUT to its
                 master, and the reason the recovery file exists at all.
      STALE      the sa3 trunk + atmf-link state is the config as it stood at
                 ~00:26:40 (the recovery file's timestamp); the saved config at
                 00:29 had sa3 as a plain access port.
  Result: DUT Role Member, Current ATMF Nodes 1; its sa3 Down/Init/Blocking.
  Master: IE520-sa absent from `show atmf nodes` (3 nodes); vlink2 RequestReset /
  Blocking. IP reachability was fine throughout -- the master pinged 10.38.215.41
  3/3 at 00:42 -- so the missing piece was the tunnel config, not the path.

=========================== FINDINGS ======================================
1. The recovery file on USB was found, retrieved and applied, but the node came back
   WITHOUT its virtual link, logged "Unable to remove recovery link", and ended in
   "Automatic node recovery failed - user intervention required".
2. Before the wipe the DUT itself reported "Special Link Not Present" although its
   virtual link was Full/Forwarding; the master reported "Special Link Present".
   If the member never registers its virtual link as a special link, a recovery
   file without the tunnel is exactly what you would expect -- candidate root cause.
3. The recovery file tracked the special-link event (00:26:40), not the saves; what
   was recovered was that snapshot, not the saved configuration.

=========================== CONFOUNDS -- read before filing =================
A. THE DUT WAS A FORMER STACK MEMBER. It held stack ID 2 (port2.0.x). The NVS erase
   reset it to ID 1, so the recovered port2.0.x lines landed on PHANTOM ports
   (`provisioned`) and sa3 stayed down. This is an artefact of how this bench made
   its standalone, and it breaks sa3 -- but it does NOT explain finding 1: the
   virtual link is IP-based, and vlan1 10.38.215.41 came back up and was reachable.
B. n = 1.
   ==> Clean repeat: renumber the standalone to ID 1 first; rebuild the virtual
       link; confirm `show atmf recovery-file` on the DUT reads "Special Link
       Present" BEFORE wiping. If it still reads "Not Present" with a Full tunnel,
       that alone reproduces finding 2 without needing the wipe.

=========================== BENCH STATE AT EXIT ===========================
DUT IE520-sa: stack ID 1 (ports now port1.0.x), recovered config in RUNNING only --
  flash holds NO default.cfg, so a reboot returns it to factory defaults (and it
  would re-read the USB recovery file). Reachable at 10.38.215.41 over its port1.0.13
  <-> stack port1.0.13; eth2 on its port1.0.2.
Master: vlink2 still configured; sa3 access mode, no atmf-link; port4.0.9 SHUT
  (unsaved) -- one sa3 leg only, deliberately, until the DUT's sa3 is rebuilt.
AMF network: master + 4050-5g + x230-10GP (3 nodes), secure mode off.
